# Project docs

`docs/` holds durable write-ups of shipped domain systems (design intent, how they work, code maps). Start with `docs/occupancy.md`, `docs/network-tier.md`, `docs/email.md`, `docs/weather.md`, and `docs/theme.md`. Architecture explorations and option spaces live in `.cursor/ideas/` (see `ideas.mdc`) — not here.

## How to treat them

- **Canonical narrative.** Prefer `docs/<topic>.md` over inventing a parallel explanation in chat, plans, or always-on rules.
- **Read when relevant.** Before changing a documented area, open the matching doc (and the short `.cursor/rules/<topic>.mdc` edit constraints if present).
- **Update in the same change.** If behaviour, pipeline, or code map drifts, edit the doc in the same session — do not leave it stale.
- **Facts, not changelog.** State current paths and behaviour. No “done on …”, phase retrospectives, or shipped success checklists.
- **Not pending work.** Open follow-ups may be listed briefly in a doc; actionable unfinished work belongs in `.cursor/plans/`. Delete completed plans; do not archive them into `docs/`.
- **Keep rules thin.** Glob-scoped rules under `.cursor/rules/` may point at `docs/…` and keep only must-preserve edit constraints. Do not duplicate the full doc into the rule.
- **README stays the entrypoint.** Operator commands (dev, sync, deploy) stay in `README.md`; `docs/` goes deeper on system design.
